T* (## 8\) Academic/policy research themes) Tj
T* (Australian scholarship and policy analysis consistently examine:) Tj
T* (- whether high thresholds and long lags reduce practical transparency) Tj
T* (- how caps and bans alter party revenue models and campaign strategies) Tj
T* (- constitutional litigation risk in design of caps/exemptions) Tj
T* (- whether reforms reduce dark money and regulatory arbitrage across jurisdictions) Tj
T* (- local-government integrity risks around property development and donor influence.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Useful recurring sources include Parliamentary Library quick guides, JSCEM inquiries/reviews, integrity) Tj
T* (body reports \(e.g., anti-corruption inquiries at state level\), and election regulator post-election) Tj
T* (disclosure/compliance reports.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (## 9\) Practical compliance checklist \(for campaigns/advocacy groups\)) Tj
T* (1. Identify election type and jurisdiction first.) Tj
T* (2. Determine whether you are a party, candidate, associated entity, third-party campaigner, donor, or) Tj
T* (publisher/broadcaster.) Tj
T* (3. Check current indexed thresholds/caps for the relevant financial year.) Tj
T* (4. Confirm whether federal purpose rules are triggered for mixed campaigns.) Tj
T* (5. Maintain contemporaneous donation source verification and expenditure records.) Tj
T* (6. Track annual, election-period, and expedited return deadlines separately.) Tj
T* (7. Reconcile local-government-specific rules where applicable.) Tj
T* (8. Obtain legal advice for cross-jurisdiction campaigns.) Tj
T* () Tj
